Chromosomal Deletion Syndrome (also called Chromosome Deletion Syndromes) is a group of genetic disorders characterized by the presence of deleted regions on chromosomes. These regions can result from deletions that are present in one or both of the parents or in the embryo during the first 10 days of development.

Deletion of these regions can affect the function or expression of specific proteins, leading to various symptoms and disorders. Some examples include:
- CRI-DI (Chromosome Drosophila Inactivating Syndrome) caused by a single deletion of the Drosophila chromosome 4.
- Wolf-16 (Wolf-Hirschhorn Syndrome) caused by a deletion of the Drosophila chromosome 5q16p21.
- Prader-Wheeler Syndrome caused by a deletion of the Drosophila chromosome 11q11.2.
- Angelman Syndrome caused by a deletion of the Drosophila chromosome 18p21.

